You were right. The tree has a common way to do this, and v7 uses it.
The new version is "[freebsd-ports PATCH v7] net-mgmt/signoz:
Observability platform for logs, metrics, and traces" [1]. Its notes
give the full reasoning and an interdiff against v6.

Why it needs doing: signoz pins bytedance/sonic v1.14.1. That release
defines a runtime map type only for Go 1.24 and 1.25, so it fails to
compile with the tree's Go 1.26 and 1.27 ("undefined:
GoMapIterator"). I reproduced that just now, outside the port. sonic
fixed it in v1.15.0, but signoz has not taken the fix; SigNoz PR
#11975, which bumps sonic, has been open since July. Separately,
sonic's loader calls mmap(2) with fd 0, which FreeBSD rejects, and
only an unreleased upstream commit fixes that.

The prior art: 22 GO_MODULE ports need a newer copy of one module, and
they fetch it with GH_TUPLE and swap it into vendor/ in pre-patch.
security/nebula does this for x/net, x/term and x/sys. v7 follows
nebula: one GH_TUPLE entry, pinned at the sonic commit that carries
both fixes, and two lines of pre-patch. That replaces post-fetch,
post-extract, SONIC_EDIT, the proxy lines, PATCHFILES and
PATCH_WRKSRC. The only port with a hand-written module download, like
v6's, is www/comentario. I should not have used it as a model. I
counted how many comparable ports use each mechanism before choosing
one this time.

> Same goes for all the workarounds in this file. You should study the
> porter's handbook and other ports to learn the common techniques for
